    "title": "Siouxlanders attempt to tag butterflies before they fly south - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Aveya Hannan Maren Ewertz ,\n1 days ago\nSIOUX CITY , Iowa ( KCAU ) — As the cooler temperatures continue to take hold of Siouxland, monarch butterflies will soon start their journey down south. But before they do, one organization is trying to tag them.\nOn Wednesday, naturalists from the Dorothy Pecaut Nature Center taught some students about how to spot and catch monarchs out at the Sioux City Prairie.\nStory continues below\nOfficails said monarch tagging is crucial for scientists to study their migration patterns. Scientists have learned that weather can have a big impact in the butterflies' migration.\n\"Best weather for the butterflies would be nice and sunny, nice and warm\" said Naturalist at the Dorothy Pecaut Nature Center Kari Leuer. \"They actually cannot fly at temperatures below 55 degrees. And if it's raining or too windy, the butterflies do not go out in that either. So, with the weather we're having today, the butterflies are probably hiding in the trees and trying to stay dry.\"\nOrganizers said the weather also played a role in how many people were able to come out to help tag the butterflies.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed.\nFor the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KCAU 9 News | Sioux City, IA | News, Weather, and Sports.",
    "summary": "By Aveya Hannan Maren Ewertz , SIOUX CITY , Iowa ( KCAU ) — As the cooler temperatures continue to take hold of Siouxland, monarch butterflies will soon start their journey down south. On Wednesday, naturalists from the Dorothy Pecaut Nature Center taught some students about how to spot and catch monarchs out at the Sioux City Prairie. Officails said monarch tagging is crucial for scientists to study their migration patterns. Scientists have learned that weather can have a big impact in the butterflies' migration.",
